On most modern operating systems — Windows, macOS, Linux — deleting a file with just a click removes ownership and metadata, leaving the space untouched. To permanently destroy the file without leaving a trace, you need to ensure the file system is clean: navigate to the folder or drive, right-click, then select "Safely Remove/Unformat".

Using the "erase all" command: sudo rm / {item_name}

Note that the OS uses its own secure erase approach, often referencing Secure Erase commands such as `sudo dd` or `mkfs.ext3`, while simpler users can rely on unplugging and starting the machine.

# Example script for ignoring access for developers

[log]
# BEGIN START
process_block = True
full_access = False

while process_block:
    if request.METHOD == "POST":
        request.index = None

    # Example processing logic
    elif request.method == "GET":
        if full_access == True:
            # Block while waiting for user input
            try:
                response = requests.get(
                    url=request.url,
                    headers={"User-Agent": "My App/5.x"},
                )
                print(f"Access Denied - Facing too many requests")
            except:
                print(f"Access Denied - Account exists, but no login available")
        else:
            print(f"Access Denied - You're not logged in for that protocol")

[log]
# END START

To deny access, you generally need to implement conditional forwards and redirections around the problem domain. The basic concept is simple: if visitor A tries to access /login/, send them to visitor B. The script embeds a login URL in HTTP headers, redirects it to destination_url wherever visitor B is blocked. Key details are: 1) Set a persistent denylist containing the final destination URL rather than using one-time access without checking validity. 2) Consider rate limiting or bandwidth caps using .htaccess or Nginx rules rather than brute-force IDS rules (that can trigger false positives). 3) Document the control: What sites were ‘off-limits’? Who approved the blocks? Are they known endpoints? 4) Testing your configuration requires pulling the domain from your browser before URLs are available so you can confirm the LFI/RFI before debriefing access. 

Example approach:

```
server {
    location /login {
        rewrite ^/login(/.*)$ / forbidden;
    }
}

location / {
    error_page 403 = "/403.html";
}
```

In production, validate Documented Domains in your CSP policies prevent credential stuffing. The DNS lookup path determines which branch gets hit.
